WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.600
Martha Gellhorn was present during every single
war and conflict around the world for 60 years.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.500
She was the only woman to land on the shores
of Normandy during D-Day.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.960
Throughout the course of her life, she was
a pacifist, and devoted her life to documenting

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.250
everything she could with a sense of perspective
and empathy for the human beings she was surrounded

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.250
by.

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:24.300
Many have called her the greatest war correspondent
who ever lived.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:34.380
Early Life and Career

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.660
Born in 1908 in St. Louis Missouri, Martha
Gellhorn could not ask for two more feminist

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:40.790
parents in the world.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:46.960
Her mom was a suffragette, her dad was a gynecologist,
so they were all about female empowerment.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.390
In 1916, her mother brought Martha to witness
7,000 people protesting at the Democratic

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:54.460
National Convention for women's right to vote
when she was just 8 year old.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.290
This event stuck with her for the rest of
her life, and young Martha never took her

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.410
vote for granted.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:03.190
The Gellhorns raised their daughter to know
that she could do anything a man could do,

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:07.030
which gave her the confidence that would help
her throughout the rest of her life.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:10.830
Martha Gellhorn attended Bryn Mawr College,
which is a private all-girl's school just

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.030
outside of Philadelphia.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.720
She dropped out before she completed her degree
in journalism, because she realized that it

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.720
was not necessary for her to start a career
in writing.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.380
Martha was able to get a few articles published
in a publication called The New Republic.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:26.530
Armed with a byline, she applied for a position
as a foreign correspondent in Paris, and got

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.480
a job at The United Press.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:33.340
In 1930, she was 22 years old, and living
a happy and romantic life in Paris.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.520
Unfortunately, she became a victim of sexual
harassment at work.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.980
When she refused to sleep with someone in
exchange for a raise, she was fired.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.520
She continued to live in Paris and sell her
articles to several magazines, including Vogue.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.840
But by 1934, she decided it was time to move
back to the United States, because she felt

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.420
that the American people needed someone to
give them a voice.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:56.979
President Roosevelt had created an organization
called the Federal Emergency Relief Administration,

00:01:56.979 --> 00:01:59.210
and Martha Gellhorn began working for them.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:03.580
She would interview upwards of 5 underprivileged
families every single day, and heard their

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:07.030
struggles about poverty, disease, and so much
more.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.930
Seeing American people live in such horrible
conditions during the Great Depression was

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:11.930
shocking.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:16.250
She wanted to let the world know that the
so-called 'American Dream' wasn't as glamorous

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:18.350
as everyone thought it was.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:22.480
Her articles were so moving, that Eleanor
Roosevelt herself read them and reached out

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.700
to become Martha Gellhorn's friend.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.210
While working at FERA, Martha encouraged a
group of workers to protest when their boss

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:30.590
was treating them unfairly.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.010
This escalated into a riot, and windows were
broken.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.850
She was fired, and they tried to label her
as a Communist.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:39.560
However, the Roosevelts were on her side,
and they helped to make sure the crooked bosses

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:40.560
were removed.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.060
After losing her job, Martha Gellhorn was
invited to live and work in the White House.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:48.110
She helped Eleanor Roosevelt with writing
her monthly column called 'My Day' for a magazine

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:50.050
called Woman's Home Companion.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:53.890
However, these topics she was writing with
the First Lady were very vapid compared to

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.960
the hard-hitting journalism that she was used
to.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.849
Gellhorn thanked the Roosevelts for their
kindness, but she wanted to move on with her

00:02:59.849 --> 00:03:00.849
career.

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:03.819
She published a book called The Trouble I've
Seen, which described the suffering of the

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:05.160
American people in the Great Depression.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.630
Deep down, she knew that she wanted to use
her writing to help people, and eventually,

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:13.440
she would get her wish.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.890
Loving Hemingway

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:22.120
Even though Martha Gellhorn had already accomplished
so much during her early writing career, much

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:26.640
of that is overshadowed by the fact that she
fell in love with the famous author Ernest

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.640
Hemingway.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:31.500
In 1936, Martha Gellhorn was in Key West Florida
on vacation with her parents when she decided

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.560
to get a drink at a bar called Sloppy Joe's.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.220
At first, she was taken aback by a loud, dirty
man who was telling wild stories and acting

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.490
like the center of attention.

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:44.260
She locked eyes with the man across the room,
and realized that this was the famous Ernest

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:45.260
Hemingway.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.040
He approached her, and they began to strike
up a conversation.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.840
Even though he did not recognize her face,
it turns out that he had, in fact, read her

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.130
articles, and her first book.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.269
They both had a very similar writing style
in the way that they cut out all of the flowery

00:03:57.269 --> 00:03:59.849
language in favor of getting to the point.

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:03.390
They also deeply cared about the struggles
of other human beings.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:06.769
By the end of their fist conversation, it
was clear that they had chemistry, and they

00:04:06.769 --> 00:04:08.390
wanted to see one another again.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:11.510
Hemingway told her that he was headed to The
Spanish Civil War to write the script of a

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.590
documentary called This Spanish Earth.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:17.419
He would eventually narrate the movie, as
well, because the actor they hired couldn't

00:04:17.419 --> 00:04:19.819
get the cadence exactly right.

00:04:19.819 --> 00:04:23.909
Martha Gellhorn has never written on the war
front before, and she secured a contract with

00:04:23.909 --> 00:04:26.680
Colliers Magazine, and followed him there
soon after.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:30.199
There were several other men on the documentary
crew, as well as other reporters who lived

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:32.620
and worked together at the Hotel Florida in
Madrid.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:36.710
No one can be sure exactly when their relationship
escalated from co-workers to lovers.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:39.370
In a lot of ways, they truly were the perfect
couple.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:44.530
There was perhaps no other man alive on the
planet who was manly enough to handle Martha

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:47.650
Gellhorn's strength, and she could keep up
with Hemingway's need for adventure.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:51.610
Over the next four years, Gellhorn and Hemingway
continued their affair while he was still

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:54.599
married to his second wife, Pauline Pfeiffer.

00:04:54.599 --> 00:04:58.300
Okay... before you go calling Martha Gellhorn
nasty names in the comments of this video,

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:01.889
you should know a bit about Ernest Hemingway's
first two marriages.

00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:04.860
Ernest Hemingway's first wife was named Hadley
Richardson.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.110
After he became a bestselling author in 1926
with The Sun Also Rise, he started receiving

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:13.150
a lot of attention, and suddenly, he had to
deal with fangirls who fell in love with his

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.370
masculine writing style.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:18.969
However, he was still very poor, and could
hardly pay for basic needs.

00:05:18.969 --> 00:05:23.020
A woman Pauline Pfeiffer grew up spoiled by
wealthy parents, and she secured the posh

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.330
gig writing fashion columns for Vogue magazine.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:29.529
She often wrote in the first-person about
attending lavish parties and fashion shows.

00:05:29.529 --> 00:05:34.669
Pfeiffer was used to getting everything she
wanted in life, and Hemingway was added to

00:05:34.669 --> 00:05:35.669
her wish list.

00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:39.289
She swooped in, claiming that she wanted to
be best friends with Hadley Richardson.

00:05:39.289 --> 00:05:43.999
The two women had almost nothing in common,
and it was truly odd that this wealthy heiress

00:05:43.999 --> 00:05:46.979
was suddenly so interested in this impoverished
couple.

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:50.129
Pauline Pfeiffer seduced Hemingway, and became
pregnant with his child.

00:05:50.129 --> 00:05:54.369
He had to divorce Hadley and married Pauline,
but spent the rest of his career bitterly

00:05:54.369 --> 00:05:57.110
writing about how much he actually hated her.

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:01.210
So, no one really felt sorry for Pauline Pfeiffer
when she had ended up being cheated on, too.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:04.960
In fact, most feel that Martha Gellhorn was
his true love, and it took marrying two of

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.309
the wrong women before he could find her.

00:06:07.309 --> 00:06:09.219
Their time spent in Spain was exhilarating.

00:06:09.219 --> 00:06:13.849
Gellhorn found this new love and compassion
for the people who were affected by war.

00:06:13.849 --> 00:06:18.289
She and Hemingway both worked in China to
cover the events of the Second Sino Japanese

00:06:18.289 --> 00:06:19.289
War.

00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:21.490
From there, she covered the Nazi occupation
of Czechoslevakia and Finland.

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:25.509
When they were not working together on assignment,
they would write letters to one another, and

00:06:25.509 --> 00:06:27.399
only meet up when their schedules aligned.

00:06:27.399 --> 00:06:31.599
They had an affair for a total of three years,
and he only grew more and more convinced that

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:34.940
this was the woman he truly wanted to marry.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:38.880
In 1939, Hemingway purchased a 15-acre estate
in Cuba called Finca Vigia for he and Martha

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:39.919
to live there together.

00:06:39.919 --> 00:06:43.860
After getting his new life settled, he officially
divorced his second wife, and he and Martha

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.979
were married in 1940.

00:06:45.979 --> 00:06:49.759
Even though they now had this beautiful house
together in a tropical paradise, Martha continued

00:06:49.759 --> 00:06:51.919
to travel the world as a war correspondent.

00:06:51.919 --> 00:06:55.830
Hemingway continued to write his novels alone
at his typewriter, and went fishing and drinking

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:56.830
with the locals.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:00.879
He was so lonely, that he began a very strange
hobby of breeding six-toed cats.

00:07:00.879 --> 00:07:06.719
He bred so many of them, that they soon overran
the estate.

00:07:06.719 --> 00:07:10.979
World War II

00:07:10.979 --> 00:07:15.240
For a few years, Martha Gellhorn and Ernest
Hemingway seemed to be very happy living together

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.530
in Cuba, enjoying the beaches and the sunshine.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:21.179
Unfortunately, their marriage eventually became
toxic when Hemingway began to ask if Martha

00:07:21.179 --> 00:07:23.470
was ever going to settle down and become more
nurturing.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:25.800
This is when things got a bit awkward.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.569
Hemingway was used to going on his wild adventures.

00:07:27.569 --> 00:07:31.899
Then, he could come home to housewives like
Hadley and Pauline who were happy to cook,

00:07:31.899 --> 00:07:33.389
clean, and look after their children.

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:36.969
He knew that being a war correspondent was
Martha's passion, but he figured that after

00:07:36.969 --> 00:07:40.809
getting married, she would calm down and become
a novelist who did all of the typical duties

00:07:40.809 --> 00:07:42.089
of a wife and mother.

00:07:42.089 --> 00:07:45.839
But her motivation for being on the front
lines never went away, and she did not want

00:07:45.839 --> 00:07:47.270
to have children with him.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:50.919
Hemingway already had several children from
his previous marriages, but there came a point

00:07:50.919 --> 00:07:54.490
where he felt that he truly was missing starting
a new family with her.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:59.129
But Martha felt that it was her duty to be
a witness to the atrocities of war, instead

00:07:59.129 --> 00:08:01.349
of staying home where it was safe.

00:08:01.349 --> 00:08:05.589
'If nobody puts it down on the record anywhere,
then the monsters win totally.

00:08:05.589 --> 00:08:10.610
It must be some place on the record, because
otherwise, they can get by with anything.

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:11.669
Does it stop anything?

00:08:11.669 --> 00:08:15.180
I don't feel that anything I have been done
is any use.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.830
But at least it is better than silence.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.939
Because if you are silent, they can re-write
it any way they want.

00:08:20.939 --> 00:08:22.330
They can make it look great afterwards.

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:25.789
So there is a point in the record.'

00:08:25.789 --> 00:08:29.810
Aside from his hobby of being a crazy cat
lady, Ernest Hemingway continued his mid-life-crisis

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:33.170
by getting into the habit of hunting for German
U-Boats off the coast of Cuba.

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:37.410
He never actually caught any, but he still
filled his boat with grenades, because had

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:42.100
the mindset that maybe one day, he could be
the hero who demolished a Nazi submarine.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:46.150
When the news arrived that the Allies were
about to storm the beaches of Normandy, Martha

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:50.100
did not waste any time securing a press pass
from Colliers Magazine.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.640
She was friends with the British author Roald
Dahl, and he helped to set her up with a sponsorship

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.960
to have her plane ticket paid by the Royal
Air Force, so long as she wrote positively

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:00.590
about them in her piece.

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:04.280
For whatever reason- Whether it was anger,
jealousy, or pure spite, Hemingway marched

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:07.390
right into Collier's, and demanded to take
her press spot.

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:11.120
Since he was the famous novelist, they couldn't
refuse the opportunity to publish one of his

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:12.120
pieces.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:16.250
They gave him Martha's plane ticket, and the
press pass.Martha was infuriated by his betrayal,

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:19.090
but she was still determined to make her way
into the war zone.

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:23.440
She dressed up as a Navy nurse, and stowed
away in the bathroom of a ship headed to Europe.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.360
Meanwhile, as Ernest Hemingway boarded a plane,
Martha Gellhorn was struggling to get to Europe

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:28.360
by sea.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:32.510
Once he arrived in Paris, Hemingway began
partying almost immediately, and he decided

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:36.870
to work together with a rag-tag group of war
correspondents who were on a mission to liberate

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:38.860
The Hotel Ritz in Paris.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:41.831
This used to be his favorite bar in the city,
and he wanted nothing more than to try to

00:09:41.831 --> 00:09:45.180
get in some Nazi-fighting action.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:49.110
Hemingway began cheating on Martha Gellhorn
with a woman he met named Mary Welsh.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:52.590
Finally, after stowing away on a ship and
risking her life, Martha Gellhorn arrived

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:55.620
at the harbor Liverpool, ready to confront
her husband.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:59.310
She heard that he was at the hospital in London,
because he had gotten into a car accident

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:00.480
while drunk driving.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:04.260
When she found Hemingway, he seemed perfectly
fine aside from a gash in his head, which

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:05.730
was wrapped in bandages.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:10.340
He was drinking whisky surrounded by fans
who were hanging on to his every word.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:13.430
Martha decided not to waste any more time
or energy on Hemingway.

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:16.730
She went to Normandy, and snuck her way onto
the front lines of D-Day.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:20.430
Since Hemingway still had her press pass,
Gellhorn had no choice but to dress as a nurse

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:24.280
and helped to carry injured soldiers on a
stretcher on and off the battlefield.

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:29.090
She paid attention to her surroundings, making
mental notes of what to write later.

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:31.740
Exposing the Truth

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:35.337
After the Allied victory, she did not go back
home right away.

00:10:35.337 --> 00:10:38.830
She stayed in Europe, and made her want to
the Dachau Concentration Camp.

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:42.410
Martha was one of the very first and only
journalists to arrive on the scene when it

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:43.930
was liberated by the Allies.

00:10:43.930 --> 00:10:47.360
No one could ever anticipate the horrors that
unfolded there.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:51.560
Martha Gellhorn was mere feet away from the
starved bodies of the Jewish prisoners lying

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.850
next to the dead German soldiers who had been
perfectly clean and well-fed.

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:59.730
She describes a horrific scene of bodies with
shaved heads that were so thin, their ages

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:02.340
or genders were indistinguishable from one
another.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:05.270
They could smell the rotting corpses.

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:09.860
The last shipment of prisoners had arrived
from Buchenwald to Dachau, but the Nazis locked

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:14.490
the train doors from the outside, and left
them to die slowly of starvation.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:17.440
Gellhorn describes a Polish prisoner they
found inside of a train car.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:20.780
'One man had survived; he was found in a pile
of the dead.

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:26.290
Now he stood on the bones that were his legs,
and talked, and suddenly wept, 'Everyone is

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:27.290
dead.'

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:28.290
'

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.130
On the plane ride out of Germany, Martha sat
together with a group of stunned American

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:32.730
prisoners of war who had been rescued from
Dachau.

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:35.930
She wrote: 'No one looked out the window as
we flew over Germany.

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:38.070
No one wanted to see Germany again.

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:43.600
They turned away from it in sickness; everything
about it was evil'' The men sat in silence

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:47.610
for a long time, until one soldier said, 'no
one will believe us.'

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:50.890
After bearing witness to some of the most
sickening atrocities in human history, it

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:55.680
only reminded Martha Gellhorn why her job
to expose the truth truly was so important.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:59.120
She knew that she needed to continue to devote
her life to this cause.

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:03.200
On her flight back home, she must have reflected
on how petty all of the arguments with her

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:04.410
husband truly was.

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.160
None of it was worth it anymore.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:09.820
After arriving back in the United States,
Martha Gellhorn filed for divorce from Ernest

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:10.820
Hemingway.

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:14.400
He was absolutely furious, and wrote letters
to her saying that she was a 'phony and pretentious

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:15.400
bitch'.

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:18.330
Then, he set out to ruin her reputation, just
like he had with his second wife.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:23.630
Their divorice was so uncivilized, she scarcely
wanted to speak to him- or even about him,

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:24.630
ever again.

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:28.310
During interviews later in life, Martha Gellhorn
refused to talk about her marriage with Hemingway.

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:30.050
"I've been a writer for over 40 years.

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:33.190
I was a writer before I met him and I was
a writer after I left him.

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:36.410
Why should I be merely a footnote in his life?"

00:12:36.410 --> 00:12:39.280
Hemingway would go on to get married to his
mistress, Mary Welsh.

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:43.000
She would be his fourth and final wife, and
she had absolutely no problem fitting into

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:44.590
the role of a homemaker.

00:12:44.590 --> 00:12:47.860
Unfortunately, even though he got the sort
of wife he wanted, Hemingway continued to

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:49.060
struggle with being happy.

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:53.490
He would eventually commit suicide in 1961.

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:58.930
Later Life and Career

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:02.380
Following her divorce from Ernest Hemingway,
Martha Gellhorn decided to remain single for

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:04.480
several years, and focused on her career.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:08.430
In 1949, she visited Rome to see children
in an orphanage who had lost limbs or became

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.280
blind because they accidently stepping on
landmines.

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:15.860
She wrote a piece called 'The Children Pay
the Price' for the Saturday Evening Post.

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:20.640
Her heart was breaking for these children,
so she chose to adopt a little boy named George

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:21.970
'Sandy' Alexander.

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:25.530
Since she was an American woman trying to
adopt an Italian boy, she had to write several

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:29.540
letters to the Roosevelts, asking for their
help to bring Sandy back to the United States,

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:31.600
so that she could provide a better life for
him.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:34.650
However, her busy schedule prevented her from
being a full-time mother.

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:39.070
Most of the time, Sandy was left in the care
of Martha's parents, and he was sent to boarding

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:40.070
school.

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:44.110
After taking some time to be on her own, Martha
began dating several successful men who were

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:45.850
doctors, businessmen, and journalists.

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:50.130
Eventually, she married the managing editor
of Time Magazine, T.S. Matthews in 1954, but

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:51.670
they also got a divorce in 1963.

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:54.500
After two failed marriages, she never tried
again.

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:58.420
The lifestyle she chose of traveling to far-off
places to put her life in danger was very

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.220
difficult to balance being a wife or mother.

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:04.960
So she remain single, traveling around the
world and reporting from the front lines,

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:09.440
because she truly felt compelled that this
is what she was supposed to be doing with

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:10.440
her life.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:14.240
When we talk about honoring people in the
armed forces, the word 'sacrifice' is often

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:15.240
said.

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:18.550
For Martha Gellhorn, she truly did sacrifice
everything to be a war correspondent.

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:22.460
She gave up her marriages, the chance to have
children, and precious time spent with friends

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:23.500
and family.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:27.320
Instead of living for herself, she chose an
existence that gave a voice to the downtrodden

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.090
across the globe.

00:14:29.090 --> 00:14:33.860
Martha often traveled alone with nothing to
her name except a single suitcase of her bare

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:34.860
essentials.

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:38.280
She wrote that loneliness was far more difficult
than anything else.

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:41.920
In one of her letters, she wrote, 'I was used
to having only myself, cold and hard as that

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:42.960
is; I could live with it.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:47.300
And now I wait, for a voice, a face, a body,
that is not is not mine, does not in any case

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:50.930
wait as I do, nor share this homesickness.'

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:54.960
After witnessing so many wars, there was nothing
that Martha Gellhorn hated more than a government

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:56.120
who was power-hungry.

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:59.560
In her book, The Face of War, she gives a
breakdown of just how much money the United

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:03.880
States spends on nuclear weapons, and at the
time she wrote the book, it amounted to over

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:05.920
$1 billion a day.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:09.320
So much could be done with that money to help
the poor and the sick, and yet it all goes

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:12.460
into these weapons that could lead to the
destruction of the human race.

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:16.710
For her, the straw that broke the camels'
back was witnessing first-hand what Americans

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:18.270
were doing during The Vietnam War.

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:22.260
It disgusted her so much that she couldn't
bring herself to continue to live in the United

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:23.260
States anymore.

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:26.450
She moved to London, which is where she spent
the rest of her life.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:30.370
In 1998, she was 89 years old, and she was
almost completely blind.

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:32.390
She was also suffering from ovarian cancer.

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.900
She was sick, and barely hanging on to life.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:39.380
So she decided to commit suicide by ingesting
a cyanide capsule, and left everything she

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:41.670
had to her adopted son, Sandy.

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:45.500
Ten years later, in 1999, the Martha Gellhorn
Prize for Journalism was established.

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:50.220
In order to win the award, a war correspondent
must accomplish reporting that '...is essentially

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:54.950
a human story that penetrates the established
version of events and illuminates an urgent

00:15:54.950 --> 00:16:00.230
issue'the unpalatable truth, validated by
powerful facts, that exposes establishment

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:02.150
conduct and its propaganda.'

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:05.600
Even after witnessing some of the darkest
parts of humanity throughout the course of

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:09.170
her career, she never gave up hope that people
can be good.

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:12.029
'I do not hope for a world at peace, all of
it, all the time.

00:16:12.029 --> 00:16:17.089
I do not believe in the perfectibility of
man, which is what would be required for world

00:16:17.089 --> 00:16:18.089
peace.

00:16:18.089 --> 00:16:19.529
I only believe in the human race.

00:16:19.529 --> 00:16:22.410
I believe the human race must continue.'

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:26.710
For years to come, Martha Gellhorn will continue
to inspire war correspondents everywhere to

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:31.961
do just that- Get on the ground, find the
truth of what people are experiencing, and

00:16:31.961 --> 00:16:33.040
tell the world.

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:36.810
By understanding one another, we can only
get closer to remaining in a world where peace

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:54.160
exists, and wars are few and far between.

